Fleet review 2026-10-06 · kind: bug · confidence: verified

2026-10-07, hub pass over the open cards. Not implemented: the fix is a different push model with public consequences, and the exact form needs the maintainer. What was established, and the design this card now waits on.

Why nothing smaller helps. The exposure of an ordinary command is the stretch between its pull and its push. A second fetch-and-compare right before the push only moves that stretch, it does not close it; and stopping to retry 'reference already exists' alone breaks the single-clone case k181 fixed, because without knowing the remote's value 'someone created this ref' and 'my sibling process pushed the same ref' look the same. The guarantee has to come from the push itself.

Proposed model.
1. A push carries only the refs that differ from the mirror (L != T) plus the tombstoned deletions, each with the mirror value as its lease: update only if the remote still holds T, create only if the remote has nothing. Refs with L == T are not sent at all, so a clone can no longer write over something it has not seen. (Since k324 the mirror is trustworthy for this: it only says what the board has taken.)
2. CLI transport: one --force-with-lease=<ref>:<T> per ref sent. Native transport: libgit2 has no lease; the exact place is git_remote_callbacks.push_negotiation, which Git::Native does not bind -- filed as p5-git-native k34. Without it the native route can only pre-check with ls-remote, which leaves a window of one round trip.
3. A lost lease is its own push outcome (neither transport failure nor rejection). sync_after then pulls -- the reconciliation parks the local version as it does today -- and the command decides.

Decisions needed from the maintainer.
a. `karr sync --push` becomes leased too (the reproduction on this card uses it). The wipe-guard and foreign-board refusals tell the user to 'republish this board with karr sync --push', which then needs an explicit force. Proposal: `karr sync --push --force`, named in both messages. New public flag.
b. What a command does after a lost lease. Proposal: `create` takes a new id for its card and pushes again, both cards exist (the card id printed is the final one; create prints after sync_after already). `pick` releases and picks the next candidate. `move`, `edit`, `handoff`, `archive`, `delete`: re-apply the same change on top of the remote's version once, and if the card no longer qualifies (claim taken by someone else, status changed away) fail with exit 1 and say what changed. Alternative: never re-apply, always fail and let the caller re-run.
c. Whether the native transport may ship with the ls-remote pre-check until Git::Native has the callback, or whether leased pushes go through the CLI until then.

Order once decided: p5-git-native k34, then Git.pm (push set, lease, outcome), SyncLifecycle (lost-lease path), the per-command handling, an ADR, the two-clone test from this card.

2026-10-25 01:33 Decisions (taken on the coordinator's delegation, measured against git 2.47.3 before deciding):

a) Push model. The board push is guarded: only refs whose local value differs from the refs/karr-remote mirror go out, each with the mirror's value as expectation; a ref the mirror lacks is create-only; deletions only for refs the mirror has. Transport: `git push --porcelain --atomic --force-with-lease=<ref>:<oid> <oid>:<ref> ...` -- the remote checks under its own ref lock, and --atomic keeps a card and its log entry together. Native route (no git binary, KARR_NO_CLI_FALLBACK) checks against a fetch taken right before the push: one round trip stays open until p5-git-native k34 binds push negotiation. That is why the card no longer waits for k34. Measured: `--force-with-lease=ref:` means must-not-exist, a failed lease reports `[rejected] (stale info)`, the others `(atomic push failed)`, and the lease holds for deletes too.

b) `karr sync --push` is guarded as well (pulls the other clone's change in, parks a conflict as any pull does, pushes again; max 3 rounds). The old forced push survives only as `karr sync --push --force`, for republishing over an empty or foreign remote; the wipe-guard and foreign-board messages name it.

c) After a stale push: each process journals the board refs it wrote since its pull. Stale refs it did not write -> pull and push again. Its own writes stale -> CAS them back to their pre-command value (id counter excepted, it only moves forward), pull; then `create` and `pick` run again from the top (next free id / next candidate, Role::ReapplyOnStale); move/edit/handoff/archive/delete and the rest exit 1, name the card and say nothing was published. Why not re-apply those: they print per card before their push, may have asked for confirmation, and act on a card the caller named -- re-applying on top of an unseen change is the silent merge.

Deviation from "Done when": 'reference already exists' still counts as contention and is retried, but the retry carries the same expectation, so if the other push landed it comes back stale instead of overwriting. Treating contention as stale outright would fail an `edit` with exit 1 when the other push did not land.

